9,2)) ... hmm, let me think more carefully.\n\nFor a given position p, the digit at position p changes only if p is one of the two chosen positions. The probability that p is chosen is: number of pairs including p / total pairs = (9-1)/C(9,2) = 8/36 = 2/9.\n\nIf p is not chosen (probability 7/9), digit stays = p.\n\nIf p is chosen, the other position q is uniformly one of the other 8 positions. The digit at position p becomes the digit from position q, which is q. So E[digit at p | p chosen] = average of q over q ≠ p = (sum of all digits - p)/8 = (45 - p)/8.\n\nE[digit at p] = (7/9)*p + (2/9)*((45-p)/8) = (7/9)*p + (2(45-p))/(72) = (7/9)*p + (45-p)/36.\n\n= (28p)/36 + (45-p)/36 = (28p + 45 - p)/36 = (27p + 45)/36 = (3(9p + 15))/36 = (9p+15)/12 = 3(3p+5)/12 = (3p+5)/4.\n\nE[N'] = sum_{p=1}^{9} ((3p+5)/4) * 10^(9-p) = (1/4) * sum_{p=1}^{9} (3p+5) * 10^(9-p).\n\nLet me compute sum_{p=1}^{9} (3p+5) * 10^(9-p).\n\np=1: 8 * 10^8 = 800,000,000\np=2: 11 * 10^7 = 110,000,000\np=3: 14 * 10^6 = 14,000,000\np=4: 17 * 10^5 = 1,700,000\np=5: 20 * 10^4 = 200,000\np=6: 23 * 10^3 = 23,000\np=7: 26 * 10^2 = 2,600\np=8: 29 * 10^1 = 290\np=9: 32 * 10^0 = 32\n\nSum = 800,000,000 + 110,000,000 + 14,000,000 + 1,700,000 + 200,000 + 23,000 + 2,600 + 290 + 32\n\n= 800,000,000 + 110,000,000 = 910,000,000\n+ 14,000,000 = 924,000,000\n+ 1,700,000 = 925,700,000\n+ 200,000 = 925,900,000\n+ 23,000 = 925,923,000\n+ 2,600 = 925,925,600\n+ 290 = 925,925,890\n+ 32 = 925,925,922\n\nE[N'] = 925,925,922 / 4 = 231,481,480.5. ✓\n\nGreat, this confirms E[N'] = 231,481,480.5 = 462,962,961/2.\n\nSo m = 462,962,961, n = 2, m+n = 462,962,963, and the remainder mod 10^6 is 962,963.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786667430}
