tfrac{2}{9}\\cdot\\tfrac{45-p}{8} = \\frac{3p+5}{4}.$$\n$$\\mathbb{E}[N'] = \\frac{1}{4}\\sum_{p=1}^{9}(3p+5)\\,10^{9-p} = \\frac{925{,}925{,}922}{4} = 231{,}481{,}480.5. \\checkmark$$\n\n**Reducing to lowest terms.** $\\mathbb{E}[N'] = \\dfrac{462{,}962{,}961}{2}$. Since $462{,}962{,}961$ is odd, $\\gcd(m,n)=1$, so $m = 462{,}962{,}961$, $n = 2$.\n\n$$m + n = 462{,}962{,}963, \\qquad 462{,}962{,}963 \\bmod 10^6 = \\boxed{962963}.$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this problem. N = 123456789, which has 9 digits, all distinct: 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 at positions 1-9 (from left, i.e., position i has digit i, with place value 10^(9-i)).\n\nWe choose two distinct positions and swap their digits. Since all digits are distinct, choosing two distinct positions = choosing two distinct digits.\n\nThere are C(9,2) = 36 pairs of positions.\n\nLet me think about the expected value. N = sum_{i=1}^{9} d_i * 10^(9-i) where d_i = i.\n\nWhen we swap positions i and j (i < j), the new number N' = N - d_i*10^(9-i) - d_j*10^(9-j) + d_j*10^(9-i) + d_i*10^(9-j).\n\nSo the change is: (d_j - d_i)*10^(9-i) + (d_i - d_j)*10^(9-j) = (d_j - d_i)(10^(9-i) - 10^(9-j)).\n\nSince d_i = i and d_j = j, the change = (j - i)(10^(9-i) - 10^(9-j)).\n\nE[N'] = N + E[change] = N + (1/36) * sum_{i<j} (j-i)(10^(9-i) - 10^(9-j)).\n\nLet me compute S = sum_{1<=i<j<=9} (j-i)(10^(9-i) - 10^(9-j)).\n\nLet me substitute a = 9-i, b = 9-j, so a > b (since i < j means 9-i > 9-j), and j-i = (9-b)-(9-a) = a-b. So S = sum over a > b >= 0, a,b in {0,...,8} of (a-b)(10^a - 10^b).\n\nHmm, let me think of it differently. Let me expand:\n\nS = sum_{i<j} (j-i) * 10^(9-i) - sum_{i<j} (j-i) * 10^(9-j).\n\nFor the first sum, fix i, sum over j from i+1 to 9: coefficient of 10^(9-i) is sum_{j=i+1}^{9} (j-i) = 1+2+...+(9-i) = (9-i)(10-i)/2.\n\nFor the second sum, fix j, sum over i from 1 to j-1: coefficient of 10^(9-j) is sum_{i=1}^{j-1} (j-i) = 1+2+...+(j-1) = j(j-1)/2.\n\nSo S = sum_{i=1}^{9} [(9-i)(10-i)/2] * 10^(9-i) - sum_{j=1}^{9} [j(j-1)/2] * 10^(9-j).\n\nLet k = 9-i in first sum (k from 0 to 8): coefficient is k(k+1)/2 * 10^k.\nLet k = 9-j in second sum (k from 0 to 8): coefficient is (9-k)(8-k)/2 * 10^k.\n\nSo S = sum_{k=0}^{8} [k(k+1)/2 - (9-k)(8-k)/2] * 10^k.\n\nLet me compute the coefficient c_k = [k(k+1) - (9-k)(8-k)] / 2.\n\nk(k+1) = k² + k\n(9-k)(8-k) = 72 - 17k + k²\n\nk(k+1) - (9-k)(8-k) = k² + k - 72 + 17k - k² = 18k - 72.\n\nSo c_k = (18k - 72)/2 = 9k - 36.\n\nSo S = sum_{k=0}^{8} (9k - 36) * 10^k = 9 * sum_{k=0}^{8} k * 10^k - 36 * sum_{k=0}^{8} 10^k.\n\nsum_{k=0}^{8} 10^k = (10^9 - 1)/9 = 111111111.\n\nsum_{k=0}^{8} k * 10^k: Let me compute. This is a known sum. \n\nLet T = sum_{k=0}^{n} k * 10^k. There's a formula. T = (10^(n+1) * (9n - 1) + 10) / 81... let me just compute directly.\n\nk=0: 0\nk=1: 10\nk=2: 200\nk=3: 3000\nk=4: 40000\nk=5: 500000\nk=6: 6000000\nk=7: 70000000\nk=8: 800000000\n\nSum = 0 + 10 + 200 + 3000 + 40000 + 500000 + 6000000 + 70000000 + 800000000\n\n= 876543210.\n\nLet me verify: 800000000 + 70000000 = 870000000, + 6000000 = 876000000, + 500000 = 876500000, + 40000 = 876540000, + 3000 = 876543000, + 200 = 876543200, + 10 = 876543210. Yes.\n\nSo S = 9 * 876543210 - 36 * 111111111.\n\n9 * 876543210 = 7888888890.\n36 * 111111111 = 3999999996.\n\nS = 7888888890 - 3999999996 = 3888888894.\n\nLet me verify: 7888888890 - 3999999996 = 3888888894. \n\nNow E[change] = S / 36 = 3888888894 / 36.\n\n3888888894 / 36: 36 * 100000000 = 3600000000, remainder 288888894. 36 * 8000000 = 288000000, remainder 888894. 36 * 24000 = 864000, remainder 24894. 36 * 691 = 24876, remainder 18. 36 * 0.5 = 18. So 108024691.5? That's not integer. Let me recheck.\n\nHmm, 3888888894 / 2 = 1944444447. 1944444447 / 18... \n\nLet me recompute. 3888888894 / 36.\n\n3888888894 / 36: \n36 * 107 = 3852, 3888 - 3852 = 36, bring down 8 → 368. 36*10 = 360, remainder 8, bring down 8 → 88. 36*2 = 72, remainder 16, bring down 8 → 168. 36*4 = 144, remainder 24, bring down 8 → 248. 36*6=216, remainder 32, bring down 8 → 328. 36*9=324, remainder 4, bring down 9 → 49. 36*1=36, remainder 13, bring down 4 → 134. 36*3=108, remainder 26.\n\nHmm, this doesn't divide evenly. Let me recheck S.\n\nWait, let me recheck. Actually let me recompute S more carefully.\n\nActually, let me recompute c_k. We need to double check.\n\nc_k = (18k - 72)/2 = 9k - 36.\n\nk=0: -36\nk=1: -27\nk=2: -18\nk=3: -9\nk=4: 0\nk=5: 9\nk=6: 18\nk=7: 27\nk=8: 36\n\nS = -36*1 - 27*10 - 18*100 - 9*1000 + 0*10000 + 9*100000 + 18*1000000 + 27*10000000 + 36*100000000\n\n= -36 - 270 - 1800 - 9000 + 0 + 900000 + 18000000 + 270000000 + 360000000\n\nNegative part: -36 - 270 - 1800 - 9000 = -11106.\nPositive part: 900000 + 18000000 + 270000000 + 360000000 = 648900000.\n\nS = 648900000 - 11106 = 648888894.\n\nHmm, that's different from before. Let me recheck.\n\nWait, I think I made an arithmetic error. Let me redo.\n\nUsing the formula S = 9 * 876543210 - 36 * 111111111.\n\n9 * 876543210: \n876543210 * 9 = 7888888890. Let me verify: 876543210 * 10 = 8765432100, minus 876543210 = 7888888890. Yes.\n\n36 * 111111111:\n111111111 * 36 = 111111111 * 4 * 9 = 444444444 * 9 = 3999999996. Yes.\n\nS = 7888888890 - 3999999996 = 3888888894.\n\nBut direct computation gives 648888894. These don't match. Let me find the error.\n\nLet me recompute the direct sum.\n\nS = sum_{k=0}^{8} (9k-36) * 10^k.\n\nk=0: (0-36)*1 = -36\nk=1: (9-36)*10 = -27*10 = -270\nk=2: (18-36)*100 = -18*100 = -1800\nk=3: (27-36)*1000 = -9*1000 = -9000\nk=4: (36-36)*10000 = 0\nk=5: (45-36)*100000 = 9*100000 = 900000\nk=6: (54-36)*1000000 = 18*1000000 = 18000000\nk=7: (63-36)*10000000 = 27*10000000 = 270000000\nk=8: (72-36)*100000000 = 36*100000000 = 360000000\n\nSum = -36 - 270 - 1800 - 9000 + 0 + 900000 + 18000000 + 270000000 + 360000000\n\nNegative: -(36 + 270 + 1800 + 9000) = -(11106) = -11106\nPositive: 900000 + 18000000 + 270000000 + 360000000\n\n900000 + 18000000 = 18900000\n18900000 + 270000000 = 288900000\n288900000 + 360000000 