o 72*10^8 = 7200000000, and 36*10^8 = 3600000000. Difference = 3600000000.\n\nBut in my direct computation I had k=8: 36*100000000 = 360000000. That's wrong! 36 * 100,000,000 = 3,600,000,000, not 360,000,000.\n\nI made an arithmetic error. 36 * 10^8 = 36 * 100,000,000 = 3,600,000,000.\n\nLet me redo the direct computation:\nk=8: 36 * 100000000 = 3,600,000,000\n\nPositive part: 900000 + 18000000 + 270000000 + 3600000000\n= 900000 + 18000000 = 18900000\n+ 270000000 = 288900000\n+ 3600000000 = 3888900000\n\nS = 3888900000 - 11106 = 3888888894. ✓\n\nGreat, so S = 3888888894.\n\nNow E[change] = S/36 = 3888888894/36.\n\nLet me divide: 3888888894 / 36.\n3888888894 / 2 = 1944444447\n1944444447 / 18 = ?\n\n18 * 100000000 = 1800000000, remainder 144444447\n18 * 8000000 = 144000000, remainder 444447\n18 * 24000 = 432000, remainder 12447\n18 * 691 = 12438, remainder 9\n18 * 0.5 = 9\n\nSo 1944444447/18 = 108024691.5. Not integer.\n\nHmm, so S is not divisible by 36? Let me check: 3888888894 / 36.\n\n3888888894 / 36: \n36 * 108000000 = 3888000000, remainder 888894.\n36 * 24000 = 864000, remainder 24894.\n36 * 691 = 24876, remainder 18.\n18/36 = 0.5.\n\nSo 3888888894/36 = 108024691 + 18/36 = 108024691.5.\n\nSo E[change] = 108024691.5 = 216049383/2.\n\nHmm wait, that means E[N'] = N + 216049383/2 = (2N + 216049383)/2 = (2*123456789 + 216049383)/2 = (246913578 + 216049383)/2 = 462962961/2.\n\nLet me check if 462962961 and 2 are coprime. 462962961 is odd, so yes.\n\nm = 462962961, n = 2. m + n = 462962963.\n\n462962963 mod 10^6 = 962963.\n\nWait, let me double-check the whole approach. Let me verify with a small example or re-derive.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The problem says \"two distinct digits are randomly chosen.\" Since all 9 digits in N are distinct (1-9), choosing two distinct digits is the same as choosing two distinct positions. There are C(9,2) = 36 equally likely pairs. Good.\n\nLet me verify the change formula with the example: swap 2 and 4 in 123456789 → 143256789